BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:yamlab-<images/yamlab/VERSION> is never pushed to a public registry (docs/protocol/README.md §2.4,
D5). Every evaluation host builds it locally; the YAMLab tasks reference that tag and Harbor finds it in the local Docker
image store.

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the pip wheels of the Isaac stack (from pypi.nvidia.com).
  NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published. Everything else in it may
  be: YAMLab (github.com/ARISE-Initiative/yamlab @ ec0455d, MIT, including the I2RT YAM robot and workstation USDs), Isaac
  Lab (github.com/isaac-sim/IsaacLab @ f4aa17f, BSD-3-Clause), YAMLab's LeRobot fork (github.com/RogerDAI1217/lerobot
  @ 67031e9, Apache-2.0; YAMLab's recorder module imports it) and the two tasks' object assets (Hugging Face dataset
  yamlab/yamlab_datasets @ fc6be973, tasks_data/{PutPotOnCooktop,HangMugOnTree}/objects, MIT).

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and every
    container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim wheels on pypi.nvidia.com;
  - the licences above (MIT, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Data
  The two tasks' object assets (USD meshes and materials, ~3 GB) are baked in, under /opt/yamlab-data. So are the web
  assets the scene would otherwise load from NVIDIA's asset bucket at run time (images/yamlab/web_assets.txt, each checked
  against its sha256, under /opt/isaac-assets): Isaac Lab's default ground and the three MDL materials of YAMLab's
  workstation (localize_assets.py rewrites the workstation USDs' URLs to the copies). A task container has no general
  network access (the agent reaches only the model APIs, the verifiers none), and Isaac Sim starts and renders offline. YAMLab's
  teleoperation demonstrations, its MimicGen-annotated demos and its generated LeRobot datasets are NOT in the image (they
  solve the tasks; the reference solutions live in the task directories' solution/ only). The frozen task instances live in
  the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 5.1 supports (Isaac
  renders with RTX even headless; PhysX runs on the CPU). Checked on an RTX 4090 (AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, driver 580.159).
  Build: ~60 GB of free disk during the build (the image is ~26.6 GB), network access to github.com, astral.sh,
  pypi.org, download.pytorch.org, pypi.nvidia.com, huggingface.co and omniverse-content-production.s3*.amazonaws.com,
  about 25-40 min. Run: one Isaac simulator per ~16 GB of host RAM (a YAMLab process peaks at ~11 GB; the standard sidecar
  and its replays run one at a time) and ~1-3 GB of GPU memory per simulator while it starts (the RTX renderer's
  initialisation fails when the GPU runs out of memory: start fewer at once on a shared GPU).

Build (from the repository root)
  docker build -f images/yamlab/Dockerfile -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:yamlab-$(cat images/yamlab/VERSION) .
  The build follows YAMLab's install.sh (torch 2.7.0+cu128, Isaac Sim 5.1.0, Isaac Lab at the commit YAMLab pins, LeRobot,
  YAMLab), then sets every Python package to images/yamlab/requirements.lock (the reference build's `uv pip freeze`) without
  resolving and fails unless the environment is exactly the lock.

Check a build
  The frozen instances must rebuild to their recorded state hashes, and a fresh-process replay of the reference trajectory
  must succeed and end in its recorded hash. With a task directory at hand:
    t=tasks/yamlab/yamlab-put-pot-on-cooktop-i00-privileged
    docker run --rm --gpus all --shm-size 8g -v $PWD/$t:/t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:yamlab-$(cat images/yamlab/VERSION) bash -c \
      "cd /tmp && cp /t/environment/rcb_yamlab.py . && python -c \"import json, numpy as np, rcb_yamlab as m; \
         s = m.Session('put_pot_on_cooktop', instance='/t/environment/instance.json'); s.record_start(); \
         [s.step(a) for a in np.load('/t/solution/oracle_actions.npy')]; s.save_trajectory(s.record_stop(), '/tmp/t.npz'); \
         print(s.status())\" && python rcb_yamlab.py replay --task put_pot_on_cooktop --instance /t/environment/instance.json --traj /tmp/t.npz"
  expects instance_exact true, success true and the same final state_sha from both runs as the task README's Verification
  section (the oracle's hash). Or run `harbor run -p tasks/yamlab/yamlab-put-pot-on-cooktop-i00-privileged -a oracle`.

On a CI machine (maintainers)
  A self-hosted runner with such a GPU builds the image with the command above when images/yamlab/ changes, runs the check,
  and keeps the image in its local Docker store (or in a registry the organization keeps private), never on a public
  registry. Hosts of the same organization may copy a built image among themselves (docker save ... | zstd, docker load); a
  new VERSION means a rebuild on every host.
